window.__courseChapter("v1-06", {"v1-06-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Network Architectures** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, Purpose", "md": "Selecting a neural network architecture is both a modeling decision and a contract with physics. The commitment begins with the structure in the data. An architecture that matches that structure can learn with fewer parameters and examples, while a poor match spends additional data and machine time compensating for the wrong computational pattern. Convolution, attention, recurrence, and embedding lookup do not merely encode different assumptions about data; they create different patterns of computation, state, and data movement."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "Architectural Principles", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "MLPs: Dense Pattern Processing", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "CNNs: Spatial Pattern Processing", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Skip Connections and Normalization", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "RNNs: Sequential Pattern Processing", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Attention: Dynamic Processing", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Transformers: Parallel Sequence Processi", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Inductive bias buys data and cost**", "Each architecture is defined by a structural assumption fixed before training (locality and weight sharing, ordered recurrence, content-based routing), and a bias that matches the data cuts parameters, samples, and operations while weak priors pay off only at large data and compute."], ["**Every family lowers onto GEMM**", "Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder."], ["**Architectures as cost signatures**", "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies."], ["**Quadratic attention**", "Dense self-attention computes sequence-length-squared score interactions, so doubling context quadruples that work, while materializing the score matrix is optional storage that tiled kernels remove, and attention arithmetic dominates a block only past a context of about six times the hidden size."], ["**Autoregressive decode and the KV cache**", "Prefill runs the whole prompt as weight-reusing GEMMs and is usually compute-bound, while decode emits one token per step at about 1 FLOP/byte, re-reading every weight plus a KV cache that grows linearly per token per request, so small-batch decode is bandwidth-bound."], ["**Embedding capacity wall**", "Embedding lookups map high-cardinality IDs to learned rows read at data-dependent addresses, so a recommender is capacity-first: table size decides where tables live (shrink, move, or partition) and which bandwidth the data term pays before arithmetic matters."], ["**Architecture selection by workload profile**", "The same accelerator is compute-, bandwidth-, or capacity-bound by architecture alone, so selection maps data symmetries to an operator family, filters candidates against storage, activation peak, intensity, latency, and power budgets, then validates on the target device."], ["**Skip connections and normalization**", "Residual connections keep gradients alive through deep stacks and normalization steadies forward scale, but the skip input stays live through the branch, residual adds and normalization are bandwidth-bound and want fusion, and BatchNorm adds batch dependence and training-serving skew."], ["**Efficient architecture families**", "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$Convolution: $output[i,j,k] = \\sum_c \\sum_{di,dj} W[di,dj,c,k] X[i+di,j+dj,c]$$$\n\n$$RNN update: $h_t = f(W_{\\text{hh}}h_{t-1} + W_{\\text{hx}}x_t + b_h)$$$\n\n$$Scaled attention: $Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK^T / \\sqrt{d_k})V$$$\n\n$$Self-attention: $SelfAttention(X) = softmax(XW_Q(XW_K)^T / \\sqrt{d_k})XW_V$$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-06-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Inductive bias buys data and cost", "back": "Each architecture is defined by a structural assumption fixed before training (locality and weight sharing, ordered recurrence, content-based routing), and a bias that matches the data cuts parameters, samples, and operations while weak priors pay off only at large data and compute."}, {"front": "Every family lowers onto GEMM", "back": "Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder."}, {"front": "Architectures as cost signatures", "back": "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies."}, {"front": "Quadratic attention", "back": "Dense self-attention computes sequence-length-squared score interactions, so doubling context quadruples that work, while materializing the score matrix is optional storage that tiled kernels remove, and attention arithmetic dominates a block only past a context of about six times the hidden size."}, {"front": "Autoregressive decode and the KV cache", "back": "Prefill runs the whole prompt as weight-reusing GEMMs and is usually compute-bound, while decode emits one token per step at about 1 FLOP/byte, re-reading every weight plus a KV cache that grows linearly per token per request, so small-batch decode is bandwidth-bound."}, {"front": "Embedding capacity wall", "back": "Embedding lookups map high-cardinality IDs to learned rows read at data-dependent addresses, so a recommender is capacity-first: table size decides where tables live (shrink, move, or partition) and which bandwidth the data term pays before arithmetic matters."}, {"front": "Architecture selection by workload profile", "back": "The same accelerator is compute-, bandwidth-, or capacity-bound by architecture alone, so selection maps data symmetries to an operator family, filters candidates against storage, activation peak, intensity, latency, and power budgets, then validates on the target device."}, {"front": "Skip connections and normalization", "back": "Residual connections keep gradients alive through deep stacks and normalization steadies forward scale, but the skip input stays live through the branch, residual adds and normalization are bandwidth-bound and want fusion, and BatchNorm adds batch dependence and training-serving skew."}, {"front": "Efficient architecture families", "back": "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Inductive bias buys data and cost**?", "options": ["Prefill runs the whole prompt as weight-reusing GEMMs and is usually compute-bound, while decode emits one token per step at about 1 FLOP/byte, re-reading every weight plus a KV cache that grows linearly per token per request, so small-batch decode is bandwidth-bound.", "Each architecture is defined by a structural assumption fixed before training (locality and weight sharing, ordered recurrence, content-based routing), and a bias that matches the data cuts parameters, samples, and operations while weak priors pay off only at large data and compute.", "Residual connections keep gradients alive through deep stacks and normalization steadies forward scale, but the skip input stays live through the branch, residual adds and normalization are bandwidth-bound and want fusion, and BatchNorm adds batch dependence and training-serving skew.", "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Inductive bias buys data and cost:** Each architecture is defined by a structural assumption fixed before training (locality and weight sharing, ordered recurrence, content-based routing), and a bias that matches the data cuts parameters, samples, and operations while weak priors pay off only at large data and compute.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Inductive bias buys data and cost itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Every family lowers onto GEMM**?", "options": ["The same accelerator is compute-, bandwidth-, or capacity-bound by architecture alone, so selection maps data symmetries to an operator family, filters candidates against storage, activation peak, intensity, latency, and power budgets, then validates on the target device.", "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution.", "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies.", "Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Every family lowers onto GEMM:** Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Every family lowers onto GEMM itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Architectures as cost signatures**?", "options": ["Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder.", "Embedding lookups map high-cardinality IDs to learned rows read at data-dependent addresses, so a recommender is capacity-first: table size decides where tables live (shrink, move, or partition) and which bandwidth the data term pays before arithmetic matters.", "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution.", "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Architectures as cost signatures:** Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Architectures as cost signatures itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Quadratic attention**?", "options": ["Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies.", "Residual connections keep gradients alive through deep stacks and normalization steadies forward scale, but the skip input stays live through the branch, residual adds and normalization are bandwidth-bound and want fusion, and BatchNorm adds batch dependence and training-serving skew.", "Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder.", "Dense self-attention computes sequence-length-squared score interactions, so doubling context quadruples that work, while materializing the score matrix is optional storage that tiled kernels remove, and attention arithmetic dominates a block only past a context of about six times the hidden size."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Quadratic attention:** Dense self-attention computes sequence-length-squared score interactions, so doubling context quadruples that work, while materializing the score matrix is optional storage that tiled kernels remove, and attention arithmetic dominates a block only past a context of about six times the hidden size.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Quadratic attention itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Autoregressive decode and the KV cache**?", "options": ["Residual connections keep gradients alive through deep stacks and normalization steadies forward scale, but the skip input stays live through the branch, residual adds and normalization are bandwidth-bound and want fusion, and BatchNorm adds batch dependence and training-serving skew.", "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution.", "The same accelerator is compute-, bandwidth-, or capacity-bound by architecture alone, so selection maps data symmetries to an operator family, filters candidates against storage, activation peak, intensity, latency, and power budgets, then validates on the target device.", "Prefill runs the whole prompt as weight-reusing GEMMs and is usually compute-bound, while decode emits one token per step at about 1 FLOP/byte, re-reading every weight plus a KV cache that grows linearly per token per request, so small-batch decode is bandwidth-bound."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Autoregressive decode and the KV cache:** Prefill runs the whole prompt as weight-reusing GEMMs and is usually compute-bound, while decode emits one token per step at about 1 FLOP/byte, re-reading every weight plus a KV cache that grows linearly per token per request, so small-batch decode is bandwidth-bound.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Autoregressive decode and the KV cache itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Inductive bias buys data and cost: Each architecture is defined by a structural assumption fixed before training (locality and weight sharing, ordered recurrence, content-based routing), and a bias that matches the data cuts parameters, samples, and operations while weak priors pay off only at large data and compute.", "Every family lowers onto GEMM: Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder.", "Architectures as cost signatures: Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies.", "Quadratic attention: Dense self-attention computes sequence-length-squared score interactions, so doubling context quadruples that work, while materializing the score matrix is optional storage that tiled kernels remove, and attention arithmetic dominates a block only past a context of about six times the hidden size."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v1-06-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-06-01-architectural-principles": {"title": "Architectural Principles", "summary": "Apply the inductive bias concept to justify a CNN-over-MLP architecture choice on structured spatial data and explain how the bias reduces per-detector parameter count while preserving useful spatial structure.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Architectural Principles"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Architectural Principles** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the inductive bias concept to justify a CNN-over-MLP architecture choice on structured spatial data and explain how the bias reduces per-detector parameter count while preserving useful spatial structure\n- Evaluate the trade-offs of inductive bias strength and justify why structural assumptions must align with data domain properties\n- Explain how architectural graph structure acts as an infrastructure commitment by setting work, state, and dependency patterns that shape the iron-law terms"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Architectural Principles", "md": "Three requests reach the same accelerator: a camera frame for an image classifier, a prompt for a language model, and a click for a recommender. The silicon is identical, yet the first request keeps the arithmetic units busy, the second leaves them waiting on memory bandwidth, and the third cannot begin until the system decides where its multi-gigabyte embedding tables can live. What differs is the network architecture: how each model arranges the same arithmetic around the structure of its data."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Inductive bias as the unifying concept mapping data structure to hardware behavior; Arithmetic intensity as the diagnostic for compute-bound vs bandwidth-bound regimes; Lighthouse workload signatures (ResNet-50, GPT-2, DLRM, MobileNetV2, KWS) and their dominant bottlenecks; Architecture choice as an infrastructure commitment locking in iron law terms."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does the chapter describe selecting a neural network architecture as 'signing a contract with physics' rather than merely selecting a mathematical modeling preference? Explain how architectural graph structure shapes or constrains the terms in the iron law of ML systems ($T_{\\text{exec}} = D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW} + O/(R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}) + L_{\\text{lat}}$) without making those terms fully fixed.", "md": "Selecting an architecture commits the system to a particular pattern of work, state, and dependencies before low-level optimization begins. A CNN tends to create local reuse, an RNN imposes ordered state updates, a transformer creates dense token interactions and KV-cache growth, and a DLRM can create capacity pressure when embedding tables exceed device memory; batching, cache behavior, precision, and kernel choices then determine how those structural commitments appear in realized data movement, utilization, and overhead. The practical consequence is that architecture choice constrains which iron-law term is likely to dominate, but it does not make memory traffic or launch overhead immutable properties of the family."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team must choose between an MLP and a CNN for classifying \\$224 × 224 pixel RGB medical images. A single dense first layer would require \\$224 × 224 × 3 = 150,528 input weights per output unit (yielding roughly 150 million weights for a 1,000-unit layer), whereas a CNN uses a shared \\$3 × 3 × 3 filter (27 weights). Using the chapter's framing of inductive bias, which statement best explains why the CNN is the superior starting point?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The chapter's point is not that CNNs are universally better, but that their locality and weight-sharing prior matches image structure well: the same detector can be reused across positions, and convolution is translation equivariant while later pooling can support invariant class labels. An answer based on greater expressiveness gets the comparison backwards, since CNNs are more constrained than MLPs, and the claim that convolutions remove learning is false because CNN filters are still learned by gradient descent.", "type": "single", "options": ["The CNN is strictly more expressive than the MLP, allowing it to approximate non-continuous functions that the Universal Approximation Theorem forbids.", "The MLP is mathematically incapable of representing any 2D spatial feature mapping due to lack of convolutional instruction support.", "The CNN eliminates gradient descent during optimization because convolutional spatial filters are deterministic, handcrafted operators.", "The CNN's spatial locality and weight-sharing prior directly matches image structure: convolution is translation equivariant, later pooling or aggregation can provide invariance for classification, and each feature detector uses far fewer weights than a dense detector."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the inductive bias concept to justify a CNN-over-MLP architecture choice on structured spatial data and explain how the bias reduces per-detector parameter count while preserving useful spatial structure."]}, {"q": "Because an inductive bias restricts the hypothesis space to a smaller set of representable functions, machine learning systems engineers should always select the architecture with the strongest possible inductive bias for every workload.", "explanation": "False. By the No Free Lunch theorem, an inductive bias only improves generalization and efficiency when its structural assumptions align with the true data-generating distribution. Imposing a strong spatial locality bias (such as a CNN) on tabular records or global language dependencies prevents the model from representing necessary non-local relationships, resulting in severe underfitting and degraded accuracy.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the trade-offs of inductive bias strength and justify why structural assumptions must align with data domain properties."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the inductive bias concept to justify a CNN-over-MLP architecture choice on structured spatial data and explain how the bias reduces per-detector parameter count while preserving useful spatial structure.", "You can now evaluate the trade-offs of inductive bias strength and justify why structural assumptions must align with data domain properties.", "You can now explain how architectural graph structure acts as an infrastructure commitment by setting work, state, and dependency patterns that shape the iron-law terms."]}], "id": "v1-06-01-architectural-principles", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-02-mlps-dense-pattern-processing": {"title": "MLPs: Dense Pattern Processing", "summary": "Apply the MLP's unrestricted connectivity pattern to explain why parameter count scales as $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}})$ and why streamed-weight inference can become bandwidth limited.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["MLPs: Dense Pattern Processing", "Representation vs. learnability", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Computational mapping", "System implications"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **MLPs: Dense Pattern Processing** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the MLP's unrestricted connectivity pattern to explain why parameter count scales as $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}})$ and why streamed-weight inference can become bandwidth limited\n- Analyze the gap between UAT's representational guarantee and practical learnability, and connect it to both sample-efficiency and systems-cost consequences for image models\n- Identify the manifold hypothesis from its description and explain how it motivates learnability in high-dimensional settings\n- Analyze a batch-1 dense-layer kernel as bandwidth-bound from a FLOP/byte signature and select batching as the intensity-raising fix rather than a compute upgrade\n- Calculate the exact MAC count and logical operand-read count of an MLP layer, and distinguish that count from physical DRAM traffic"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § MLPs: Dense Pattern Processing", "md": "Assuming the least about the input means letting every feature interact directly with every hidden unit. The MNIST digit classifier of a later section is the purest example of that dense processing. Each of its 784 pixel inputs connects to every hidden unit, and each hidden unit connects to every unit in the next layer. The same all-to-all pattern suits tabular inputs, such as the sender reputation, link count, and keyword features a spam filter scores, where no feature has a fixed neighbor."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Representation vs. learnability\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Computational mapping\n- System implications\n\nPay attention to: Dense connectivity and unrestricted feature interaction scaling; Universal Approximation Theorem (UAT) vs practical learnability gap; Batch-1 GEMV arithmetic intensity and the 0.5 FLOP/byte bandwidth wall; Nested loop structure and Tensor Core tile alignment penalties."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A team cites the Universal Approximation Theorem (UAT) to argue that a wide 3-layer MLP should be used to classify \\$256 × 256 RGB images instead of a CNN. Explain why UAT does not justify this design choice in practice, detailing both the statistical failure mode (sample complexity) and the systems failure mode (memory bandwidth and parameter explosion).", "md": "UAT is only an existence result: it says a sufficiently large MLP can represent a target function, not that gradient descent will find it efficiently or that the required width will be practical. For images, flattening \\$256 × 256 RGB inputs into 196,608 features removes the spatial prior, so the model must learn locality and reuse from data that a matched CNN encodes structurally; sample needs therefore depend on the target distribution and learning procedure, and a CNN can need fewer examples when that prior matches the task. On the systems side, connecting 196,608 inputs to 4,096 hidden units creates about 805 million weights, which is useful for capacity planning, and under a streamed-weight batch-1 model leaves low arithmetic intensity unless batching or cache reuse changes the traffic pattern."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A fully connected layer connecting 2,048 input units to 2,048 output units stores approximately 4.19 million weights (~16.8 MB in FP32). When applied to high-resolution image inputs, dense layers suffer severe parameter explosion. Which statement best captures the systems mechanism behind the MLP's parameter and memory scaling?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The chapter defines MLPs by the absence of a specific spatial or temporal prior, so the layer must materialize the full $d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}}$ matrix. That fixes dense parameter storage by dimensions, and in small-batch streamed-weight inference it can also make weight traffic scale with the same matrix size. The bias-vector explanation is too small to dominate, and an answer focused on activation memory confuses a conditional runtime effect with the architectural source of the parameter count.", "type": "single", "options": ["Dense layers use non-linear activations whose element-wise memory footprints dwarf the weight tensors by several orders of magnitude.", "MLP bias vectors grow quadratically with the output dimension, dominating total layer memory storage.", "The MLP encodes no specific spatial or temporal connectivity pattern, so every one of the $d_{\\text{in}}$ inputs can connect to every one of the $d_{\\text{out}}$ outputs, yielding $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}})$ weights and, when weights stream for batch-1 inference, comparable weight-memory traffic per sample.", "Dense layers require storing three master copies of every weight matrix in hardware registers during inference forward passes."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply the MLP's unrestricted connectivity pattern to explain why parameter count scales as $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}})$ and why streamed-weight inference can become bandwidth limited."]}, {"q": "The ____ hypothesis states that high-dimensional real-world data (such as natural images) actually resides on a much lower-dimensional structured surface embedded within the full input space, helping explain why useful structure can be learned from finite data.", "explanation": "manifold. The manifold hypothesis proposes that high-dimensional data lies near a lower-dimensional structured region in the ambient space, which helps explain why learning can exploit structure without searching the full input space.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["manifold"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the manifold hypothesis from its description and explain how it motivates learnability in high-dimensional settings."]}, {"q": "A single dense layer (\\$2,048 × 2,048) running FP32 inference on an A100 GPU at batch size 1 achieves only ~4% of peak compute throughput, with profilers reporting an arithmetic intensity of approx 0.5 FLOP/byte. What is the most effective engineering solution to move this kernel out of the memory-bandwidth-bound regime and raise hardware utilization?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. At batch size 1, each weight is read from memory to compute a single multiply-accumulate (GEMV), yielding $I \\approx \\frac{2 \\cdot M \\cdot N}{4 \\cdot M \\cdot N} = 0.5\\text{ FLOP/byte}$ in FP32. Batching $B$ samples together transforms the kernel into a GEMM ($\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}$ where $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{B \\times M}$), performing \\$2 \\cdot B \\cdot M \\cdot N$ FLOPs for the same \\$4 \\cdot M \\cdot N$ weight bytes read (ignoring activation traffic), which scales arithmetic intensity linearly with $B$ and pushes execution into the compute-bound regime. An unvectorized scalar loop would severely degrade SIMD instruction throughput. Upgrading peak TFLOP/s leaves memory bandwidth unchanged and does not solve a bandwidth bottleneck. Eliminating the matrix multiplication would change the underlying mathematical function of the network.", "type": "single", "options": ["Increase the batch size ($B > 1$), transforming the matrix-vector multiplication (GEMV) into a matrix-matrix multiplication (GEMM), which amortizes weight loading across $B$ samples and scales arithmetic intensity.", "Replace the dense matrix multiplication with an unvectorized scalar loop to avoid GPU kernel launch overhead.", "Upgrade to an accelerator with double the peak FP32 TFLOP/s while keeping the batch size at 1.", "Replace the linear transformation with an element-wise activation function to eliminate all weight memory traffic."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze a batch-1 dense-layer kernel as bandwidth-bound from a FLOP/byte signature and select batching as the intensity-raising fix rather than a compute upgrade."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: In the nested loop implementation of an MLP forward pass (`for batch`, `for out`, `for in_`), calculate the exact number of multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations and logical operand reads required to compute 128 hidden neurons from a 784-dimensional MNIST input vector at batch size 1. In your count, treat the scalar loop as reading inputs and weights only, explicitly excluding bias traffic and output writes, and explain why this count is not the same as the number of DRAM transactions. Also explain how framework-level BLAS libraries optimize this pattern.", "md": "For 128 outputs and 784 inputs at batch size 1, the layer performs \\$784 × 128 = 100,352 MACs, or 200,704 FLOPs if each MAC is counted as 2 FLOPs. At the scalar-loop level, each output neuron reads 784 input values and 784 weights, for 1,568 logical operand reads per neuron and 200,704 across the layer, explicitly excluding bias reads and output writes; as the text notes, that is not the same as 200,704 DRAM transactions because caches, registers, and tiling can satisfy many logical reads from faster memory after reuse. The practical consequence is that BLAS libraries improve throughput by blocking, vectorizing, and reusing this regular pattern so more operand reads are served from fast memory tiers rather than repeatedly fetched from DRAM."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the MLP's unrestricted connectivity pattern to explain why parameter count scales as $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{in}} \\times d_{\\text{out}})$ and why streamed-weight inference can become bandwidth limited.", "You can now analyze the gap between UAT's representational guarantee and practical learnability, and connect it to both sample-efficiency and systems-cost consequences for image models.", "You can now identify the manifold hypothesis from its description and explain how it motivates learnability in high-dimensional settings.", "You can now analyze a batch-1 dense-layer kernel as bandwidth-bound from a FLOP/byte signature and select batching as the intensity-raising fix rather than a compute upgrade."]}], "id": "v1-06-02-mlps-dense-pattern-processing", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-03-cnns-spatial-pattern-processing": {"title": "CNNs: Spatial Pattern Processing", "summary": "Calculate how convolutional weight sharing changes parameter count, and distinguish per-detector comparisons from non-equivalent whole-output comparisons.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["CNNs: Spatial Pattern Processing", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Computational mapping", "System implications", "Depthwise separable convolutions"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **CNNs: Spatial Pattern Processing** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate how convolutional weight sharing changes parameter count, and distinguish per-detector comparisons from non-equivalent whole-output comparisons\n- Compare translation equivariance and invariance, and justify their respective roles in intermediate feature extraction versus final classification\n- Explain the operational sequence of the im2col transformation in lowering 2D convolutions to matrix multiplications\n- Evaluate the fallacy that FLOP count directly equals inference latency and explain why arithmetic intensity dictates hardware speedup\n- Explain depthwise separable convolution components and explain how factorizing spatial and channel mixing reduces arithmetic complexity"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § CNNs: Spatial Pattern Processing", "md": "The MLP's assumption that every input may interact directly with every output proves particularly costly for spatially structured data like images. Connecting every pixel of a high-resolution input to every hidden unit creates a parameter footprint that exhausts accelerator memory and drives arithmetic intensity so low that the layer becomes bandwidth bound. The earlier MNIST comparison showed the remedy at small scale. A shared filter needs … fewer weights per feature detector than a dense unit that treats every pixel independently."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Computational mapping\n- System implications\n- Depthwise separable convolutions\n\nPay attention to: Parameter sharing and local connectivity parameter reduction; Translation equivariance ($f(\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{x})) = \\mathcal{T}(f(\\mathbf{x}))$) vs translation invariance; Receptive field growth mechanics across stacked convolutional layers; im2col lowering transformation to GEMM and memory-duplication trade-off."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Distinguish between translation equivariance ($f(\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{x})) = \\mathcal{T}(f(\\mathbf{x}))$) and translation invariance ($f(\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{x})) = f(\\mathbf{x})$). Explain why intermediate convolutional layers must maintain equivariance for object detection while final classification layers often apply global average pooling to achieve invariance.", "md": "Translation equivariance means that shifting the input shifts the output feature map by the exact same spatial offset, preserving precise positional and geometric relationships ('eye above nose'). Invariance means that transforming the input produces an identical, unchanging output, discarding spatial coordinates. Intermediate layers in object detection must remain equivariant so that downstream bounding box predictors can accurately localize object coordinates $(x, y, w, h)$. Final classification layers introduce invariance (via global average pooling) because the class label ('dog') must remain unchanged regardless of where the object appears in the frame."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A \\$3 × 3 convolutional layer with 64 input channels and 64 output channels processes a \\$224 × 224 feature map, producing 64 spatial output channels. How does its parameter count compare with a dense layer that maps the flattened \\$224 × 224 × 64 input to 64 scalar outputs, and why are the outputs not shape-equivalent?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Convolution shares one \\$3 \\times 3$ detector per input-output channel pair across all spatial positions, so its parameter count depends on kernel size and channels, not image resolution. The dense comparison reaches about 205 million parameters only because it maps the flattened \\$224 \\times 224 \\times 64$ input to 64 scalar outputs, which is not the same output shape as 64 spatial feature maps. An answer claiming the counts are equal ignores weight sharing, while the 9-parameter answer forgets that channel mixing still needs separate weights.", "type": "single", "options": ["The CNN requires \\$205\\text{ million}$ parameters, whereas the dense layer requires only \\$36{,}864$ parameters due to flattened matrix vectorization.", "The CNN requires \\$3 × 3 × 64 × 64 = 36,864 parameters (~37K), whereas a dense layer mapping a \\$224^2 × 64 input to 64 scalar outputs requires \\$224^2 × 64 × 64 approx 205 million parameters; the output shapes are not equivalent.", "Both architectures require exactly the same number of parameters because both perform 64-to-64 channel transformations.", "The CNN requires 9 parameters because spatial weight sharing reduces all kernel weights across all channels to a single \\$3 × 3 matrix."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate how convolutional weight sharing changes parameter count, and distinguish per-detector comparisons from non-equivalent whole-output comparisons."]}, {"q": "Because MobileNetV2 requires roughly 14× fewer FLOPs than ResNet-50 per \\$224 × 224 image, it is guaranteed to execute at least 10× faster on any data center GPU.", "explanation": "False. FLOPs measure arithmetic work, not execution latency. MobileNetV2 uses depthwise separable convolutions that have low arithmetic intensity and small channel dimensions per kernel, which can fail to saturate dense matrix units (such as Tensor Cores) on server GPUs. Because memory access and operator launch overhead can dominate compute, MobileNetV2 may achieve less than proportional speedup or even run slower than ResNet-50 on high-end GPUs.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the fallacy that FLOP count directly equals inference latency and explain why arithmetic intensity dictates hardware speedup."]}, {"q": "A depthwise separable convolution decomposes standard convolution into two sequential operations: a ____ convolution that applies spatial filters to each input channel independently, followed by a \\$1 × 1 pointwise convolution that projects and mixes channels across the depth dimension.", "explanation": "depthwise. Depthwise separable convolution factorizes standard convolution into a depthwise convolution (spatial filtering per channel) and a pointwise convolution (\\$1 × 1 cross-channel linear combination).", "type": "fill", "answer": ["depthwise"], "hints": ["Think about: explain depthwise separable convolution components and explain how factorizing spatial and channel mixing reduces arithmetic complexity."]}, {"q": "In a deep CNN using stacked \\$3 × 3 convolutional filters with stride 1 and padding, by how much does the receptive field side length increase with each additional layer, and what is the architectural implication for detecting large objects?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Each successive \\$3 \\times 3$ stride-1 convolutional layer expands the receptive field side length by $K - 1 = 2$ pixels (layer 1 sees \\$3 \\times 3$, layer 2 sees \\$5 \\times 5$, layer 3 sees \\$7 \\times 7$). To detect large objects spanning 100+ pixels in a \\$224 \\times 224$ image, networks must either use deep stacks of layers, strided convolutions, or pooling operations. The claim of 9 pixels confuses kernel area with linear side growth. The claim of exponential doubling is incorrect for stride-1 layers. The claim that receptive field remains \\$3 \\times 3$ confuses single-layer kernel size with cumulative receptive field depth.", "type": "single", "options": ["Receptive field increases by 9 pixels per layer, allowing a 3-layer network to cover an entire \\$224 × 224 image.", "Receptive field side length doubles with each layer, scaling exponentially as \\$3^L.", "Receptive field side length grows linearly by 2 pixels per layer (a 3-layer stack sees a \\$7 \\times 7$ region), requiring deep stacks of layers or downsampling (pooling/striding) to detect objects spanning \\$100+$ pixels in high-resolution images.", "Receptive field remains strictly fixed at \\$3 × 3 across all layers because convolutional filter weights are shared across positions."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate receptive field growth across stacked convolutional layers and analyze the depth-versus-downsampling trade-off in network design."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate how convolutional weight sharing changes parameter count, and distinguish per-detector comparisons from non-equivalent whole-output comparisons.", "You can now compare translation equivariance and invariance, and justify their respective roles in intermediate feature extraction versus final classification.", "You can now explain the operational sequence of the im2col transformation in lowering 2D convolutions to matrix multiplications.", "You can now evaluate the fallacy that FLOP count directly equals inference latency and explain why arithmetic intensity dictates hardware speedup."]}], "id": "v1-06-03-cnns-spatial-pattern-processing", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-04-skip-connections-and-normalizati": {"title": "Skip Connections and Normalization", "summary": "Analyze how identity skip connections change the block Jacobian to improve gradient conditioning in deep networks without guaranteeing perfect unit gain.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Skip Connections and Normalization", "Skip connections and gradient flow", "Normalization and activation stability"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Skip Connections and Normalization** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze how identity skip connections change the block Jacobian to improve gradient conditioning in deep networks without guaranteeing perfect unit gain\n- Compare BatchNorm and LayerNorm in terms of reduction axes, batch-size sensitivity, and training-versus-inference behavior"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Skip Connections and Normalization", "md": "When plain convolutional networks are stacked beyond roughly 20 layers, adding more layers can make *training* error worse rather than better. Gradients vanish or explode on the way back through the stack, and the scale of intermediate activations drifts on the way forward. Convolution made deep vision networks affordable in parameter count, and two building blocks made them trainable. Skip connections keep gradients alive through dozens of layers, and normalization keeps the activations those layers produce within a stable numerical range."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Skip connections and gradient flow\n- Normalization and activation stability\n\nPay attention to: Residual identity paths and gradient flow; Memory and bandwidth cost of skip connections and normalization."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Compare Batch Normalization (BatchNorm) and Layer Normalization (LayerNorm) along two critical systems dimensions: (a) sensitivity to mini-batch size during training, and (b) operational differences between training and inference (including training-serving skew).", "md": "BatchNorm depends on batch statistics during training, so small batches can make those estimates noisy; for CNN activations it reduces over batch and spatial positions, not just batch alone. LayerNorm instead normalizes each sample independently across its feature dimension, so its behavior does not depend on mini-batch size. The practical consequence is that BatchNorm also changes behavior between training and inference by switching to running statistics, which can create training-serving mismatch, whereas LayerNorm uses the same per-sample computation in both modes."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In a residual block implementing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}) + \\mathbf{x}$, how does the additive identity shortcut mathematically improve gradient conditioning through the layer Jacobian $\\mathbf{J} = \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{y}}{\\partial \\mathbf{x}}$ during backpropagation in very deep networks?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Residual addition changes the block Jacobian from an arbitrary transformation to an identity-plus-residual form. When the residual branch stays small, that factor remains near the identity, so gradient propagation is better conditioned than in a plain deep stack. The shortcut does not force zero weights or remove backpropagation; it provides a direct path that improves conditioning, although those near-identity factors can still compound across depth rather than guaranteeing perfect unit gain.", "type": "single", "options": ["The shortcut forces the residual function $\\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x})$ to have zero weights, turning the network into an immutable linear identity operator.", "The Jacobian takes the form $\\mathbf{J} = \\mathbf{I} + \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{F}}{\\partial \\mathbf{x}}$, so when residual-branch derivatives are small the block stays near identity and gradients have a direct additive path, improving conditioning without guaranteeing exact unit gain at arbitrary depth.", "The shortcut doubles the singular values of the weight matrix at every layer, ensuring gradients explode exponentially rather than vanish.", "The shortcut eliminates the backpropagation chain rule by replacing gradient updates with forward-only finite differences."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how identity skip connections change the block Jacobian to improve gradient conditioning in deep networks without guaranteeing perfect unit gain."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze how identity skip connections change the block Jacobian to improve gradient conditioning in deep networks without guaranteeing perfect unit gain.", "You can now compare BatchNorm and LayerNorm in terms of reduction axes, batch-size sensitivity, and training-versus-inference behavior."]}], "id": "v1-06-04-skip-connections-and-normalizati", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-05-rnns-sequential-pattern-processi": {"title": "RNNs: Sequential Pattern Processing", "summary": "Compare the memory complexity of RNN inference state with ordinary BPTT training memory, and distinguish retained activations from possible recomputation strategies.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["RNNs: Sequential Pattern Processing", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Gated recurrence", "Computational mapping", "System implications"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **RNNs: Sequential Pattern Processing** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the memory complexity of RNN inference state with ordinary BPTT training memory, and distinguish retained activations from possible recomputation strategies\n- Analyze why faster hardware can shorten recurrent step time without removing the sequential dependency chain, and contrast that with transformer prefill parallelism\n- Justify edge and streaming use cases where RNN constant-state memory is superior to transformer memory scaling\n- Explain the operational dataflow and matrix operations executed within a single RNN time step\n- Analyze how repeated Jacobian products in BPTT can contract or amplify gradients across long recurrent horizons"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § RNNs: Sequential Pattern Processing", "md": "An MLP requires a fixed-width input vector and a CNN sees only a bounded spatial window, so neither can process a continuous audio stream or a sentence of arbitrary length without padding, truncating, or stacking layers until activation memory overflows. Where convolutional networks exploit *spatial* structure, many real-world signals carry *temporal* structure, in which sequential order and prior context determine meaning. Recurrent networks process these variable-length streams within a fixed memory budget by carrying and updating a persistent state vector from one time step to the next."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Gated recurrence\n- Computational mapping\n- System implications\n\nPay attention to: Temporal inductive bias and hidden state recurrence $\\mathbf{h}_t = f(\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hh}}\\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hx}}\\mathbf{x}_t + \\mathbf{b}_h)$; $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ inference state memory vs $\\mathcal{O}(S)$ serial latency critical path; Backpropagation through time (BPTT) and vanishing/exploding gradient products of Jacobians; Batch parallelism vs un-parallelizable sequence-dimension dependency."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why upgrading an accelerator from 10 TFLOP/s to 100 TFLOP/s can reduce the elapsed time per recurrent step in an RNN processing a single long sequence, but cannot reduce the number of ordered state updates on the critical dependency chain. Contrast this with the parallel sequence processing capability of a transformer.", "md": "An RNN still has to execute the recurrence in order: step $t$ depends on the hidden state produced at step $t-1$, so the dependency chain still contains $S$ ordered state updates even if faster hardware shortens the wall-clock time of each step. Kernel fusion can cut launch overhead and within-step work such as input projections can exploit parallel hardware, but those improvements do not remove the ordered state-update chain or make the dependency depth collapse. By contrast, a transformer's full-sequence training or prefill path can process all positions together, replacing the recurrent time-step chain with parallel operations across sequence positions."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An RNN processes a sequence of length $S = 1{,}000$ tokens with hidden state dimension $d_{\\text{hidden}} = 128$. Which statement correctly describes the scaling of its inference state memory versus its training activation memory?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. During inference, the recurrent update only needs the current hidden state, so runtime state is constant with respect to sequence length. Under ordinary full BPTT, training retains intermediate activations across all $S$ steps so gradients can be propagated backward through time, which makes memory grow as $\\mathcal{O}(S \\cdot d_{\\text{hidden}})$. The quadratic answer confuses RNNs with dense attention, and saying this is a lower bound ignores that recomputation or checkpointing can trade extra work for less retained memory.", "type": "single", "options": ["Inference state memory is $\\mathcal{O}(d_{\\text{hidden}})$ (constant $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ with respect to sequence length $S$), whereas training with backpropagation through time (BPTT) requires storing activations across all steps, scaling as $\\mathcal{O}(S \\cdot d_{\\text{hidden}})$ under ordinary retained-state BPTT.", "Both inference state memory and training activation memory scale quadratically as $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ due to recurrent hidden-to-hidden weight matrices.", "Inference state memory scales linearly as $\\mathcal{O}(S \\cdot d_{\\text{hidden}})$, while training memory is constant because weights are shared across all time steps.", "Inference requires zero memory because recurrent states are discarded immediately after computing output probabilities."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the memory complexity of RNN inference state with ordinary BPTT training memory, and distinguish retained activations from possible recomputation strategies."]}, {"q": "Because transformers offer superior parallelization and representational capacity for long-range dependencies, recurrent neural networks are entirely obsolete and have no valid deployment use cases in modern ML systems.", "explanation": "False. For streaming inference on resource-constrained microcontrollers (TinyML) and always-on audio devices with strict power and memory budgets, an RNN's constant $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ state memory footprint (e.g., 2 KiB for a 512-dimensional FP32 state) is vastly superior to the linear KV-cache growth and quadratic attention requirements of transformers, making RNNs a systems-justified choice.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: justify edge and streaming use cases where RNN constant-state memory is superior to transformer memory scaling."]}, {"q": "Order the mathematical and dataflow operations executed during a single time-step forward pass of a standard Elman RNN cell:", "explanation": "The correct order is (2) -> (1) -> (3) -> (4) -> (5) (or (1) and (2) computed in parallel -> (3) -> (4) -> (5)). Step 1 and 2 project the input $\\mathbf{x}_t \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hx}}$ and previous hidden state $\\mathbf{h}_{t-1} \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hh}}$. Step 3 is (3) Sum the projections with bias $\\mathbf{b}_h$. Step 4 is (4) Apply $\\tanh$ activation to produce $\\mathbf{h}_t$. Step 5 is (5) Project $\\mathbf{h}_t$ via $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{yh}}$ to compute output $\\mathbf{y}_t$.", "type": "order", "items": ["Multiply the current input vector $\\mathbf{x}_t$ by the input weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hx}}$", "Multiply the previous hidden state vector $\\mathbf{h}_{t-1}$ by the recurrent weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hh}}$", "Sum the recurrent contribution, input contribution, and hidden bias vector $\\mathbf{b}_h$", "Multiply the new hidden state $\\mathbf{h}_t$ by the output weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{yh}}$ to produce output $\\mathbf{y}_t$"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the operational dataflow and matrix operations executed within a single RNN time step."]}, {"q": "In a standard RNN layer with input dimension $d_{\\text{in}} = 100$ and hidden state dimension $d_{\\text{hidden}} = 128$, how many total multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations are performed per sequence step to compute the unactivated hidden state?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. At each time step, the RNN performs two distinct matrix multiplications: the recurrent projection h_t-1 W_hh requires \\$128 × 128 = 16,384 MACs, and the input projection x_t W_hx requires \\$100 × 128 = 12,800 MACs. Together, these sum to \\$16,384 + 12,800 = 29,184 MACs per step per batch item. The choice of 12,800 MACs omits the recurrent projection. The choice of 1,280,000 MACs incorrectly assumes quadratic all-to-all history computation. The choice of 256 MACs confuses dimension addition with full matrix transformations.", "type": "single", "options": ["12,800 MACs, because only the input projection performs matrix multiplication.", "29,184 MACs, consisting of \\$128 \\times 128 = 16{,}384\\text{ MACs}$ for the recurrent projection plus \\$100 \\times 128 = 12{,}800\\text{ MACs}$ for the input projection.", "1,280,000 MACs, because recurrence multiplies all hidden states across all past time steps simultaneously.", "256 MACs, because an RNN updates only a single vector addition per step."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the per-step multiply-accumulate arithmetic cost of input and recurrent projections in an RNN layer."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: During backpropagation through time (BPTT) over $S$ time steps, the gradient of the loss with respect to the initial hidden state satisfies $\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\mathbf{h}_0} \\propto \\prod_{t=1}^S \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{h}_t}{\\partial \\mathbf{h}_{t-1}}$. Explain the mathematical mechanism that causes gradients to vanish or explode as $S$ grows large.", "md": "The gradient is a product of Jacobians across many recurrent steps, and for the column-vector recurrence in the text one step has the form $\\mathbf{J}_t = \\operatorname{diag}(f'(\\mathbf{z}_t))\\,\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hh}}$. If these repeated transformations contract the propagated directions, the product shrinks and long-range gradients vanish; if they repeatedly expand the propagated directions, the product grows and gradients can explode. The practical consequence is that simple RNNs can lose distant information or become numerically unstable over long horizons, even though a single step may look well behaved."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare the memory complexity of RNN inference state with ordinary BPTT training memory, and distinguish retained activations from possible recomputation strategies.", "You can now analyze why faster hardware can shorten recurrent step time without removing the sequential dependency chain, and contrast that with transformer prefill parallelism.", "You can now justify edge and streaming use cases where RNN constant-state memory is superior to transformer memory scaling.", "You can now explain the operational dataflow and matrix operations executed within a single RNN time step."]}], "id": "v1-06-05-rnns-sequential-pattern-processi", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-06-attention-dynamic-processing": {"title": "Attention: Dynamic Processing", "summary": "Explain why scaling attention scores by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ helps control score magnitude and softmax saturation in scaled dot-product attention.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Attention: Dynamic Processing", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Computational mapping", "System implications"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Attention: Dynamic Processing** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain why scaling attention scores by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ helps control score magnitude and softmax saturation in scaled dot-product attention\n- Calculate the memory footprint of materialized attention score matrices and analyze quadratic context scaling\n- Compare exact tiled attention with naive attention by separating dense arithmetic cost from score-storage and memory-traffic cost\n- Explain the three core projection components (Q, K, V) of the attention mechanism and describe their roles in content-based routing\n- Calculate the exact multiply-accumulate operation count required for pairwise query-key score computation in an attention head"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Attention: Dynamic Processing", "md": "Recurrent networks serialize execution along the sequence dimension and compress all historical context into a fixed-capacity hidden state vector (a later section). In a sentence such as \"The cat, which was sitting by the window overlooking the garden, was sleeping,\" the subject \"cat\" and predicate \"sleeping\" are separated by ten intervening words, yet they form the core syntactic and semantic relationship. An RNN must process all intervening tokens sequentially, risking information loss across intermediate recurrent transitions while preventing hardware from parallelizing across time steps."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Computational mapping\n- System implications\n\nPay attention to: Content-dependent dynamic routing ($\\text{Attention}(\\mathbf{Q},\\mathbf{K},\\mathbf{V}) = \\text{softmax}(\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^T/\\sqrt{d_k})\\mathbf{V}$); $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ path length between any two tokens vs $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ score computation; Naive score materialization ($S \\times S$ scores per head, quadratic memory wall) vs FlashAttention IO tiling; Attention scaling factor $\\sqrt{d_k}$ and numerical stability / variance control."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Consider a single transformer self-attention layer processing a sequence of length $S = 4{,}096$ with $N_{\\text{heads}} = 12$ attention heads in FP16 precision (2 bytes per score). Calculate the memory required to store the materialized attention score matrices $(\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^T)$ for this single layer, and explain why doubling the context length to $S = 8{,}192$ creates a super-linear memory wall.", "md": "For S = 4,096 and 12 heads, the number of score elements in one layer is S × S × N_heads = 4,096 × 4,096 × 12 = 201,326,592 elements. At 2 bytes per element (FP16), this consumes \\$201,326,592 × 2 = 402,653,184 bytes approx 402.7 MB per layer (or ~33.6 MB per head). Because dense score interactions scale quadratically as mathcalO(S^2), doubling the context length from 4,096 to 8,192 increases the score elements by a factor of (8,192/4,096)^2 = 4×, requiring approx 1.61 GB per layer. Across 32 retained layers during training, materialized score storage explodes from ~12.9 GB to ~51.5 GB, quickly exceeding available accelerator SRAM/HBM capacity."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why does scaled dot-product attention divide the query-key dot product $\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^T$ by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ prior to applying the softmax normalization function?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. In this section, the taught point is that larger head dimensions can make raw dot products grow in magnitude, which would push softmax toward saturation and make learning harder. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ reins in those score magnitudes before normalization. An answer about changing $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ complexity confuses numerical scaling with algorithmic structure, and scaling also does not make keys unnecessary.", "type": "single", "options": ["To convert the matrix multiplication into a sparse graph lookup that reduces compute complexity from $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(S)$.", "Scaling limits the magnitude of attention scores as $d_{\\text{head}}$ grows, helping keep softmax from saturating too sharply; it does not remove quadratic score computation or guarantee universal overflow protection.", "To force the sum of all elements in the unnormalized query-key matrix to equal exactly 1.0 before applying softmax.", "To eliminate the need for Key weight matrices by making Query and Value representations mathematically identical."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain why scaling attention scores by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ helps control score magnitude and softmax saturation in scaled dot-product attention."]}, {"q": "IO-aware algorithms like FlashAttention reduce the computational complexity of dense self-attention from $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ down to $\\mathcal{O}(S)$ floating-point operations.", "explanation": "False. FlashAttention keeps dense self-attention's quadratic arithmetic because it still computes the full set of exact score interactions. Its gain is avoiding full score-matrix storage in HBM by tiling the computation and applying online softmax, so the memory-traffic benefit depends on the kernel and memory hierarchy rather than changing the dense arithmetic to linear in $S$.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: compare exact tiled attention with naive attention by separating dense arithmetic cost from score-storage and memory-traffic cost."]}, {"q": "In the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, the input sequence is projected into three distinct learned representations known as Queries, Keys, and ____, drawing a direct analogy to content-addressable retrieval systems.", "explanation": "Values (or values). Attention projects inputs into Queries, Keys, and Values, where Queries match Keys to compute weights that aggregate Values.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Values", "Values (or values)"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the three core projection components (Q, K, V) of the attention mechanism and describe their roles in content-based routing."]}, {"q": "In an attention layer with sequence length $S = 512$ and per-head feature dimension $d_k = 64$, how many multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations are required to compute the query-key attention scores $(\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^T)$ for a single attention head, excluding softmax normalization and value aggregation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Computing the score matrix $\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^T$ for a single head involves multiplying an $S \\times d_k$ matrix by a $d_k \\times S$ matrix. This produces an $S \\times S$ output matrix where each of the \\$512 \\times 512 = 262{,}144$ entries requires a $d_k = 64$ dimensional dot product. The total arithmetic cost is \\$512 \\times 512 \\times 64 = 16{,}777{,}216\\text{ MACs}$ (approx. 16.8M MACs). The choice of 32,768 MACs computes only a single vector-matrix projection. The choice of 262,144 MACs counts output matrix elements without multiplying by feature depth $d_k$.", "type": "single", "options": ["32,768 MACs, calculated as \\$512 × 64.", "262,144 MACs, calculated as \\$512 × 512.", "1,048,576 MACs, calculated as \\$512 × 512 × 4.", "16,777,216 MACs (~16.8 million MACs), calculated as $S \\times S \\times d_k = 512 \\times 512 \\times 64$."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the exact multiply-accumulate operation count required for pairwise query-key score computation in an attention head."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain why scaling attention scores by $\\sqrt{d_{\\text{head}}}$ helps control score magnitude and softmax saturation in scaled dot-product attention.", "You can now calculate the memory footprint of materialized attention score matrices and analyze quadratic context scaling.", "You can now compare exact tiled attention with naive attention by separating dense arithmetic cost from score-storage and memory-traffic cost.", "You can now explain the three core projection components (Q, K, V) of the attention mechanism and describe their roles in content-based routing."]}], "id": "v1-06-06-attention-dynamic-processing", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-07-transformers-parallel-sequence-p": {"title": "Transformers: Parallel Sequence Processing", "summary": "Explain why unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant and justify the role of positional information in sequence modeling.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Transformers: Parallel Sequence Processing", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Computational mapping", "System implications"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Transformers: Parallel Sequence Processing** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain why unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant and justify the role of positional information in sequence modeling\n- Calculate the arithmetic intensity of batch-1 autoregressive decoding and evaluate why memory bandwidth bottlenecks single-token generation\n- Explain the architectural layout and sub-layer execution order of a standard Transformer encoder block\n- Calculate the per-request Key-Value (KV) cache memory footprint and analyze how concurrency shifts serving memory bottlenecks from weights to activation state\n- Evaluate why highly optimized batch-1 autoregressive decoding can still fall far below peak compute throughput under a low-intensity streamed-weight regime"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Transformers: Parallel Sequence Processing", "md": "Attention provides the computational primitive of dynamic, content-dependent routing between positions, yet it was originally layered on top of recurrent architectures, inheriting their sequential bottleneck. The transformer architecture removes recurrence and makes attention the operation that mixes information between positions in every layer, which enables parallel computation across sequence positions during full-sequence processing, such as training or prompt processing (reading a user's whole input at once), while retaining dynamic connectivity."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Computational mapping\n- System implications\n\nPay attention to: Parallel sequence processing and elimination of recurrence; Multi-Head Attention dimension partitioning and subspace specialization; Positional encodings to break permutation invariance; Autoregressive decoding bandwidth bottleneck ($I \\approx 1\\text{ FLOP/byte}$) vs prefill compute saturation."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Under a weight-only memory model in FP16 precision, an autoregressive language model generates 1 token per forward pass at batch size 1, performing approximately 2 FLOPs per parameter while streaming the entire weight matrix from High Bandwidth Memory (HBM). Calculate the theoretical arithmetic intensity of this decoding step and explain why it causes accelerator matrix units (Tensor Cores) to remain severely underutilized.", "md": "In FP16 precision, each parameter occupies 2 bytes, so 2 FLOPs per streamed parameter gives an arithmetic intensity of \\$1.0\\text{ FLOP/byte}$. For a hypothetical accelerator with matching FP16 compute throughput of 312 TFLOP/s and HBM bandwidth of 2.0 TB/s, the ridge point is approximately \\$156\\text{ FLOP/byte}$. The streamed-weight model therefore has a bandwidth roofline of 2.0 TFLOP/s, far below the compute ceiling. This bound explains why compute throughput can remain low; it does not measure Tensor Core idle time or prove that a particular implementation saturates HBM. Realized traffic, cache reuse, launch overhead, and available parallelism require profiling."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why does position-free unmasked self-attention require explicit positional information supplied by mechanisms such as sinusoidal signals, learned positional embeddings, or related position-encoding schemes?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The section states that unmasked self-attention treats the sequence as an unordered set unless position information is injected: if the rows of $\\mathbf{X}$ are permuted, the output rows are permuted the same way. That is permutation equivariance, not invariance, and it is why positional information from embeddings, sinusoidal signals, or related mechanisms is needed to represent order. The hardware-padding and complexity answers are unrelated to the mathematical issue.", "type": "single", "options": ["Because matrix multiplication hardware cannot process tensors without fixed static padding across all dimensions.", "Because layer normalization removes the mean and variance of token vectors, destroying word identity.", "Because without positional information, unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant: permuting the input rows permutes the output rows in the same way, so the layer by itself does not encode order.", "Because positional encodings reduce the computational complexity of the attention matrix from $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(S)$."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain why unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant and justify the role of positional information in sequence modeling."]}, {"q": "Order the sub-layer operations executed within a single standard Transformer encoder block during a forward pass:", "explanation": "The correct order is (1) -> (2) -> (3) -> (4) -> (5). Step 1 is (1) Linear Q, K, V projections. Step 2 is (2) Multi-head scaled dot-product attention. Step 3 is (3) First residual addition and layer normalization. Step 4 is (4) Position-wise feed-forward MLP. Step 5 is (5) Second residual addition and layer normalization.", "type": "order", "items": ["Project input activations into Query, Key, and Value tensors via linear weight matrices", "Compute multi-head scaled dot-product self-attention across all sequence positions", "Apply residual skip connection addition and layer normalization to the attention output", "Apply residual skip connection addition and layer normalization to the feed-forward output"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural layout and sub-layer execution order of a standard Transformer encoder block."]}, {"q": "During autoregressive language model inference, single-token generation at batch size 1 achieves near-peak GPU floating-point throughput (TFLOP/s) because the matrix-vector multiplication is highly optimized.", "explanation": "False. The chapter's streamed-weight batch-1 decode model has low arithmetic intensity, so optimization alone does not guarantee near-peak compute throughput. Whether decode is bandwidth bound depends on the machine balance and realized traffic, and prefill or larger batches can reuse weights more effectively than single-token batch-1 generation.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why highly optimized batch-1 autoregressive decoding can still fall far below peak compute throughput under a low-intensity streamed-weight regime."]}, {"q": "In a Multi-Head Attention layer with model dimension $d_{\\text{model}} = 768$ and $N_{\\text{heads}} = 12$ heads, what is the per-head dimension $d_k$, and how does multi-head projection affect total computational FLOP complexity compared to a single attention head operating on the full 768 dimensions?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Multi-head attention partitions the model dimension across heads such that $d_k = d_{\\text{model}} / N_{\\text{heads}} = 768 / 12 = 64$. For $N_{\\text{heads}}$ heads, the $S \\times S$ score calculation requires $N_{\\text{heads}} \\times (S \\times S \\times d_k) = S^2 \\times (N_{\\text{heads}} \\cdot d_k) = S^2 \\cdot d_{\\text{model}}$ MACs, which is mathematically identical to the arithmetic cost of a single full-width attention head while providing the representational capacity of diverse attention subspaces. The claim that $d_k = 768$ misstates the per-head dimension. The claim of $d_k = 12$ inverts the head count and head dimension. The claim that multi-head attention eliminates the output projection matrix is false because $\\mathbf{W}^O$ is required to mix the concatenated head outputs.", "type": "single", "options": ["The per-head dimension is d_k = 768, increasing total projection FLOPs by \\$12× compared to a single head.", "The per-head dimension is $d_k = 768 / 12 = 64$; running 12 heads of dimension 64 has the exact same total projection and score FLOP complexity as a single head of dimension 768, while enabling the model to jointly attend to information from 12 distinct representation subspaces.", "The per-head dimension is d_k = 12, reducing total computational complexity by \\$64×.", "Multi-head attention eliminates the output projection matrix $\\mathbf{W}^O$, halving layer parameter count."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the dimension partitioning mechanics of multi-head attention and explain why multi-head projection preserves total computational complexity."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A production serving system deploys a 32-layer transformer with 32 attention heads, head dimension $d_{\\text{head}} = 128$, and context length $S = 2{,}048$ in FP16 precision (2 bytes per value). Calculate the memory footprint of the Key-Value (KV) cache for a single user request, and explain why KV-cache memory can surpass model weight memory under high concurrent batching.", "md": "For a single request, the KV cache stores key and value tensors across all layers: Memory = N_L × 2 × N_heads × S × d_head × bytes = 32 × 2 × 32 × 2,048 × 128 × 2 bytes = 1,073,741,824 bytes = 1.07 GB (exactly 1.0 GiB). While a 7B parameter FP16 model weight footprint is fixed at ~14 GB, serving 32 concurrent user requests requires \\$32 × 1.07 GB approx 34.3 GB of dynamic KV cache, more than double the static weight memory. This linear growth with concurrency and context length makes the KV cache the dominant serving memory bottleneck."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain why unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant and justify the role of positional information in sequence modeling.", "You can now calculate the arithmetic intensity of batch-1 autoregressive decoding and evaluate why memory bandwidth bottlenecks single-token generation.", "You can now explain the architectural layout and sub-layer execution order of a standard Transformer encoder block.", "You can now calculate the per-request Key-Value (KV) cache memory footprint and analyze how concurrency shifts serving memory bottlenecks from weights to activation state."]}], "id": "v1-06-07-transformers-parallel-sequence-p", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-08-sparse-architectures-recommendat": {"title": "Sparse Architectures: Recommendation Systems", "summary": "Explain the architectural function and computational role of the Interaction Layer in DLRM recommendation models.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Sparse Architectures: Recommendation Systems", "Pattern processing needs", "Algorithmic structure", "Computational mapping and system implications"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Sparse Architectures: Recommendation Systems** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain the architectural function and computational role of the Interaction Layer in DLRM recommendation models\n- Analyze why recommendation workloads can hit a capacity wall before arithmetic throughput matters, and identify the architectural responses that follow\n- Analyze and sequence the four main pipeline stages of DLRM inference dataflow\n- Calculate embedding table capacity requirements and formulate systems strategies (sharding, offloading) to bypass the memory capacity wall\n- Explain why sparse embedding lookups weaken spatial locality and prefetching, while popularity skew can still create useful cache reuse for hot rows"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Sparse Architectures: Recommendation Systems", "md": "Coupling sparse lookups with dense interaction layers is the defining pattern of industrial recommendation engines. When a user opens a streaming service or e-commerce platform, the system must select a handful of recommendations from a catalog of millions within an end-to-end budget of tens of milliseconds. Meeting that target requires mapping both users and items to dense vectors in a shared embedding space and scoring their interactions at scale, which exposes a regime governed first by memory capacity."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pattern processing needs\n- Algorithmic structure\n- Computational mapping and system implications\n\nPay attention to: High-cardinality categorical features and lack of geometric structure in raw IDs; Embedding tables as memory capacity walls (TB+ scale); DLRM architecture pipeline: Bottom MLP (dense) + Embedding Lookups (sparse) -> Interaction Layer (dot products) -> Top MLP; Memory capacity bound vs memory bandwidth bound (random gathers vs dense compute)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why a large-table recommendation scenario becomes memory-capacity-bound rather than compute-bound when the model's combined embedding tables exceed the fast memory of a single accelerator, and why the standard execution form of the Iron Law of ML Systems ($T_{\\text{exec}} = D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW} + O/(R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}) + L_{\\text{lat}}$) cannot directly determine whether that model can be deployed on one accelerator.", "md": "DLRM becomes capacity bound when its combined embedding tables exceed the fast memory of one device, so the first question is not how quickly arithmetic runs but whether the model state can reside locally at all. In that regime, compression, offloading to host or remote memory, or partitioning across devices become the architectural options before runtime optimization of the dense MLPs matters. The practical consequence is that the iron-law execution terms only apply cleanly after placement is feasible, because capacity decides where the tables live and therefore which bandwidth the data term must pay."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In the Deep Learning Recommendation Model (DLRM) architecture, what is the primary computational role of the Interaction Layer?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. In DLRM, continuous numerical features are processed through a dense Bottom MLP, while categorical features are looked up in sparse embedding tables. The Interaction Layer takes these resulting dense vectors and computes all-to-all pairwise dot products, explicitly capturing interactions between numerical context and categorical embeddings before passing the concatenated results to the Top MLP. Convolutions are vision operators that do not apply to unstructured IDs. Batch normalization does not compute cross-feature dot product interactions. Autoregressive token decoding is a language model mechanism.", "type": "single", "options": ["It applies 2D convolutions over user and item IDs to extract hierarchical spatial features.", "It normalizes categorical IDs across the batch using running mean and variance statistics.", "It performs autoregressive token decoding to predict the next search query.", "It computes pairwise dot products between the dense feature representations from the Bottom MLP and the sparse embedding vectors gathered from categorical tables to capture explicit feature interactions."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural function and computational role of the Interaction Layer in DLRM recommendation models."]}, {"q": "Order the four primary computational stages executed during an end-to-end inference pass in a DLRM recommendation model:", "explanation": "The correct order is (1) and (2) in parallel -> (3) -> (4) (or (1) -> (2) -> (3) -> (4)). Step 1 and 2 are (1) Continuous features through Bottom MLP and (2) Categorical ID embedding table lookups. Step 3 is (3) Pairwise dot product feature interactions. Step 4 is (4) Top MLP classification for click-through rate prediction.", "type": "order", "items": ["Process continuous numerical features through the dense Bottom MLP to produce a dense representation", "Look up sparse categorical IDs across embedding tables to gather discrete embedding vectors", "Concatenate interaction dot products with Bottom MLP features and pass through the Top MLP to predict click-through probability"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze and sequence the four main pipeline stages of DLRM inference dataflow."]}, {"q": "Why do sparse embedding table lookups in recommendation workloads resist standard hardware caching and memory prefetching mechanisms that accelerate CNNs and MLPs?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The chapter contrasts sparse recommendation lookups with the regular contiguous or strided access of dense layers and convolutions: IDs select arbitrary rows, so spatial locality and prefetching help much less. But caches are not universally defeated, because popular users or items recur and create temporal locality for hot rows. The answers about encryption, tensor contractions, or disabled caching invent mechanisms the section does not describe.", "type": "single", "options": ["Because each incoming request queries arbitrary, non-contiguous row indices determined by sparse user and item IDs, producing irregular gathers with weak spatial locality, although popularity skew can still create temporal reuse for hot rows.", "Because embedding tables are permanently encrypted in DRAM, preventing hardware prefetchers from reading address buses.", "Because embedding lookups require performing high-order tensor contractions that stall CPU prefetch queues.", "Because recommendation systems execute only on storage-class memory where hardware caching is disabled by operating system kernels."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why sparse embedding lookups weaken spatial locality and prefetching, while popularity skew can still create useful cache reuse for hot rows."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: An e-commerce recommendation system maintains an item embedding table with 100 million items (\\$10^8$) and a user embedding table with 1 billion users (\\$10^9$), each using 128-dimensional FP32 vectors (4 bytes per parameter). Calculate the memory footprint of each table, verify why they cannot fit on a single 80 GB A100 GPU, and describe two systems strategies to handle this capacity wall.", "md": "The item table requires \\$10^8 × 128 × 4 bytes = 51.2 GB. The user table requires \\$10^9 × 128 × 4 bytes = 512.0 GB. Combined, they require \\$563.2 GB, which exceeds the 80 GB capacity of an A100 by >7× (even the item table alone consumes ~64% of an 80 GB GPU). Two systems strategies to resolve this capacity wall: (1) Model-parallel table sharding, where embedding tables are partitioned across the memory of multiple GPUs or cluster nodes; (2) Hierarchical memory offloading, where frequently accessed embeddings are cached in GPU HBM while the vast majority of cold embeddings reside in host CPU DRAM or NVMe storage."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain the architectural function and computational role of the Interaction Layer in DLRM recommendation models.", "You can now analyze why recommendation workloads can hit a capacity wall before arithmetic throughput matters, and identify the architectural responses that follow.", "You can now analyze and sequence the four main pipeline stages of DLRM inference dataflow.", "You can now calculate embedding table capacity requirements and formulate systems strategies (sharding, offloading) to bypass the memory capacity wall."]}], "id": "v1-06-08-sparse-architectures-recommendat", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-09-comparing-and-choosing-architect": {"title": "Comparing and Choosing Architectures", "summary": "Use the workload-profile assumptions to predict why transformer prefill and decode can land on different sides of the iron law.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Comparing and Choosing Architectures", "Arithmetic intensity across workloads", "Workload profiles", "Shared building blocks", "Architecture selection framework", "Architecture selection in practice"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Comparing and Choosing Architectures** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Use the workload-profile assumptions to predict why transformer prefill and decode can land on different sides of the iron law\n- Explain how weight reuse determines whether data movement dominates an architecture family's energy per inference\n- Explain the iterative scale-down loop in the Architecture Selection Decision Framework when models breach memory or latency ceilings\n- Apply the chapter's deployment-screening framework to justify why MobileNetV2 is a candidate for validation in the wildlife edge scenario\n- Analyze what arithmetic-intensity values do and do not imply under a roofline model, and identify the additional measurement needed before classifying a convolution as compute bound"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Comparing and Choosing Architectures", "md": "The three requests that opened this chapter can now be costed in numbers. A single ResNet-50 frame performs about … of arithmetic against … of FP32 weights, and each convolutional filter is reused across its feature map, creating opportunities to keep arithmetic units busy. A single GPT-2 token at batch size one performs about … against … of FP16 weights. Streaming those weights once gives a weight-only arithmetic intensity of roughly …, making bandwidth a likely limit."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Arithmetic intensity across workloads\n- Workload profiles\n- Shared building blocks\n- Architecture selection framework\n- Architecture selection in practice\n\nPay attention to: Workload profile: parameters, activations, intensity regime, parallel axis, and limiting iron-law term per family (prefill vs. decode); How weight reuse sets each family's energy cost behind the intensity column; Portable building blocks and their costs: residual gradient conditioning, BatchNorm vs. LayerNorm; Decision framework scale-down loop and the wildlife monitoring case study."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In the wildlife monitoring edge deployment case study (50 species classification on a 2 W battery-powered Cortex-A53 device with 512 MB RAM and a <500 ms latency target), explain why MobileNetV2 (0.75 width multiplier with INT8 quantization) was selected as the candidate to validate over ResNet-50 and KWS DS-CNN.", "md": "MobileNetV2 was selected as the candidate because its compact weight footprint and lower operation count make it plausible for the device budget. ResNet-50's FP32 model weights alone exceed the chapter's 100 MB model allocation within the 512 MB total system budget before accounting for activations, runtime overhead, and the rest of the application, while its heavier compute load separately pressures latency and energy. The DS-CNN keyword-spotting model is not rejected because a 200K-parameter model can never classify 50 species, but because its published workload is an audio task, so its reported accuracy and latency do not validate this visual deployment. The practical consequence is that the chapter treats MobileNetV2's roughly 209 ms estimate on the assumed throughput as a screening calculation, then requires target-device measurement of end-to-end latency, peak memory, thermal behavior, seasonal battery qualification, full-system energy, and representative task accuracy before deployment."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "The workload profile gives the transformer two rows, prefill and decode, even though both run the same weights. Under the table's representative reuse and streaming assumptions, which statement correctly explains why the limiting iron-law term can differ between them at small batch size?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The table is explicit that these are representative regimes under stated assumptions, not universal bottlenecks. In prefill, processing many prompt tokens together can reuse weights across the sequence and raise effective intensity enough for the compute term to matter; in small-batch decode, one-token-at-a-time execution rereads weights and accesses the KV cache with much lower reuse, so the data term is often the limiter. The capacity answer overstates a special case, and the serial-latency answer confuses decode with recurrent processing.", "type": "single", "options": ["Prefill is limited by the data term because it must read the whole prompt from storage, while decode is limited by the compute term because each token requires a full forward pass.", "Prefill processes all $S$ prompt tokens together, which can raise effective reuse and arithmetic intensity enough for the compute term to dominate under the stated assumptions; decode processes one token at a time with reread weights and a growing KV cache, so the data term often dominates at small batch.", "Both phases are limited by memory capacity, because the KV cache always exceeds device memory.", "Decode is compute bound because its $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ attention scores dominate, while prefill is latency bound because tokens are processed serially."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: use the workload-profile assumptions to predict why transformer prefill and decode can land on different sides of the iron law."]}, {"q": "The chapter notes that the intensity column of the workload profile doubles as an energy column. Which statement correctly explains why a batch-one MLP or transformer decode step spends far more energy per useful operation than a convolutional layer, even on the same accelerator?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Moving a 32-bit value from DRAM costs far more energy than one multiply-accumulate, so what decides a family's energy is how many operations each loaded weight feeds. A batch-one dense layer or decode step streams every weight once per request and spends most of its energy moving it; a convolution amortizes each filter load over the whole feature map, so the locality that makes it compute bound also makes it energy efficient. The first two choices misplace the cost in arithmetic, and the third ignores the difference in reuse.", "type": "single", "options": ["The MLP and decode step perform more multiply-accumulates per weight than a convolution, so their arithmetic energy is higher.", "The CNN's spatial locality lets it skip most of its multiply-accumulates, so it performs less arithmetic overall.", "All three load weights from DRAM once per batch, so their energy per operation is the same; only latency differs.", "The MLP and decode step load each weight from DRAM and use it about once per request, so DRAM traffic dominates their energy, while a convolution reuses each filter load across every position of its feature map and amortizes that movement."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain how weight reuse determines whether data movement dominates an architecture family's energy per inference."]}, {"q": "In the systematic Architecture Selection Decision Framework, if a candidate model fails the inference speed or memory budget constraint check on the target device, the engineer must immediately abandon on-device edge execution and route all inference to a cloud data center.", "explanation": "False. The Architecture Selection Decision Framework provides an iterative 'Scale Down' loop: when a model breaches memory or latency constraints, the team should first apply model compression (such as INT8 quantization, pruning, or structural width multipliers) or evaluate a more efficient architectural variant (such as MobileNet instead of ResNet) before abandoning local edge deployment.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain the iterative scale-down loop in the Architecture Selection Decision Framework when models breach memory or latency ceilings."]}, {"q": "A production profiler reveals that a model's embedding tables consume over 1 TB of memory across cluster nodes, inference requests perform sparse random row lookups rather than dense matrix multiplies, and accelerator compute units remain over 95% idle. Which lighthouse archetype best represents this workload's dominant system constraint?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. A terabyte-scale parameter footprint dominated by sparse, random embedding table lookups with largely idle compute units is the hallmark signature of DLRM (Deep Learning Recommendation Model). DLRM models are memory-capacity-bound and memory-latency-bound, requiring model-parallel table sharding across cluster memory. The ResNet-50 archetype represents compute-bound dense convolutions with high arithmetic intensity. The GPT-2 archetype represents bandwidth-bound autoregressive decoding dominated by streaming weights per token. The MobileNetV2 archetype represents latency-constrained mobile vision with depthwise separable convolutions.", "type": "single", "options": ["DLRM, because the limiting constraint is memory capacity for terabyte-scale embedding tables accessed via sparse, irregular memory gathers.", "ResNet-50, because it stresses dense matrix floating-point throughput across regular convolutional grids.", "GPT-2, because autoregressive decoding is the canonical memory-bandwidth-limited serving workload.", "MobileNetV2, because depthwise-separable convolutions produce low arithmetic intensity on server GPUs."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify a production workload by matching its profile signature (table size, access pattern, compute utilization) to the correct lighthouse archetype."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A dense MLP layer running batch-1 FP32 inference reports an arithmetic intensity of approx 0.5 FLOP/byte, while an image convolution bottleneck layer achieves >50 FLOP/byte on the same accelerator. Using the roofline model and an accelerator ridge point of \\$150 FLOP/byte, explain what follows from these numbers and what additional convolution measurement is needed before calling the convolution compute bound. Also explain why upgrading to an accelerator with double the peak TFLOP/s but unchanged memory bandwidth will not speed up the batch-1 MLP.", "md": "The batch-1 MLP is clearly below the \\$150\\text{ FLOP/byte}$ ridge point, so under this streamed-weight model it is in the bandwidth-bound regime. The convolution's $>50\\text{ FLOP/byte}$ is higher, but it still does not by itself prove a compute-bound regime because values above 50 can still lie below the \\$150\\text{ FLOP/byte}$ ridge; the next measurement is the convolution's effective realized intensity, including the traffic pattern for that kernel on this machine, and then comparing it directly with the ridge point. The practical consequence is that doubling peak TFLOP/s without raising memory bandwidth will not help the batch-1 MLP, because its limit is bytes delivered per second, not arithmetic ceiling."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now use the workload-profile assumptions to predict why transformer prefill and decode can land on different sides of the iron law.", "You can now explain how weight reuse determines whether data movement dominates an architecture family's energy per inference.", "You can now explain the iterative scale-down loop in the Architecture Selection Decision Framework when models breach memory or latency ceilings.", "You can now apply the chapter's deployment-screening framework to justify why MobileNetV2 is a candidate for validation in the wildlife edge scenario."]}], "id": "v1-06-09-comparing-and-choosing-architect", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Analyze the pitfall of budgeting transformer serving memory from weights alone and analyze KV cache scaling with concurrency and context length.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-06", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Network Architectures* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the pitfall of budgeting transformer serving memory from weights alone and analyze KV cache scaling with concurrency and context length\n- Analyze how changing execution regime can move the same transformer architecture between compute-leaning and bandwidth-leaning operating points\n- Evaluate why improved benchmark accuracy in a CNN-attention hybrid does not guarantee preserved throughput or memory efficiency\n- Analyze the fallacy that training cluster success transfers to edge hardware and evaluate total runtime memory components"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Network Architectures, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Architecture choice is a systems decision, not a leaderboard selection. The common mistakes in architecture selection arise when teams treat architecture families as interchangeable accuracy tools while ignoring inductive bias, memory traffic, hardware mapping, and deployment state."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: Fallacy: More complex architectures always perform better than simpler ones; Pitfall: Selecting architectures based solely on accuracy metrics without analyzing computational requirements; Fallacy: An architecture has one dominant bottleneck across training and inference; Pitfall: Combining architectural patterns without analyzing interaction effects at the system level."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain the fallacy: 'An architecture has one dominant bottleneck across training and inference.' Use the Transformer architecture to illustrate how execution regime (full-sequence training/prefill vs. batch-1 autoregressive decoding) shifts the primary hardware bottleneck.", "md": "The fallacy is treating a bottleneck as a fixed property of the architecture rather than a property of the architecture running in a specific regime. In a transformer's training or prefill path, many sequence positions are processed together, which can create large matrix-matrix operations and much higher effective reuse; in small-batch autoregressive decoding, the same weights are reused poorly because generation proceeds one token at a time and repeatedly streams weights and KV-cache state. The practical consequence is that exact tiling, batching, cache behavior, and machine balance can shift whether compute or bandwidth sets the limit, so each regime must be measured separately."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why is estimating LLM transformer serving memory based solely on static model parameter footprint (e.g., 14 GB for a 7B FP16 model) a critical engineering pitfall in production deployments?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Serving large language models requires memory for both static model weights and dynamic activation state. During autoregressive decoding, the system stores key and value vectors for all prior tokens in the KV cache ($B \\times N_L \\times 2 \\times N_{\\text{heads}} \\times S \\times d_{\\text{head}} \\times \\text{bytes}$). For a 7B model (14 GB weights), 32 concurrent users at 2,048 tokens require ~34.3 GB of KV cache alone, more than double the model weight footprint. The claim of 10x graph expansion is incorrect. Optimizer states are stored during training, not inference. Model weights remain resident in GPU memory during serving and are not reloaded from disk per token.", "type": "single", "options": ["Because model weights expand by \\$10× in memory due to framework compilation graph overhead.", "Because inference requires storing three full optimizer states (momentum and variance buffers) in GPU RAM.", "Because transformers delete their weights after processing each token and must reload them from disk.", "Because autoregressive decoding dynamically accumulates a Key-Value (KV) cache that scales linearly with context length and concurrency ($\\mathcal{O}(B \\times S)$), which at high concurrency or long context windows can rival or exceed the static weight memory."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the pitfall of budgeting transformer serving memory from weights alone and analyze KV cache scaling with concurrency and context length."]}, {"q": "Because a hybrid neural network architecture combining convolutional layers with self-attention achieves higher top-1 accuracy on a benchmark leaderboard, it is guaranteed to maintain the high throughput and low memory traffic of the pure CNN baseline.", "explanation": "False. The chapter's warning is that hybrid accuracy does not predict hybrid system behavior. Dense attention operates on regular contiguous tensors and does not inherently destroy CNN locality, but adding it can still increase score computation, intermediate traffic, and runtime cost. Throughput and memory efficiency therefore have to be profiled for the combined design rather than inferred from the CNN baseline.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why improved benchmark accuracy in a CNN-attention hybrid does not guarantee preserved throughput or memory efficiency."]}, {"q": "A vision model trained on a cluster of \\$8 × A100 GPUs (640 GB total memory) achieves state-of-the-art accuracy. Why is assuming this model will deploy successfully to an edge device such as an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (16 GB memory) a dangerous fallacy, even if the model weights require only 8 GB?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Model weight storage is only one component of runtime memory. During inference, the system must also allocate memory for intermediate activation feature maps, framework execution workspaces, CUDA runtime context, and operating system buffers. An 8 GB model on a 16 GB edge device leaves only 8 GB of shared system RAM; high-resolution inputs or concurrent streams can cause activation memory to breach this budget, triggering out-of-memory crashes. The assertions regarding floating-point incompatibility, permanent 8-way GPU communication hardcoding, and cloud-only execution are factually false.", "type": "single", "options": ["Because total runtime memory during inference includes intermediate activation tensors, workspace scratchpads, and operating system buffers; under high batch sizes or high input resolutions, these activation and workspace buffers easily exceed the remaining 8 GB memory ceiling.", "Because edge devices are mathematically incapable of executing the floating-point multiplication instructions used by server GPUs.", "Because models trained on 8 GPUs permanently hardcode an 8-way tensor parallel communication protocol that fails if fewer than 8 physical GPUs are connected.", "Because PyTorch and TensorFlow models can only run on cloud-hosted Linux kernels and cannot execute on embedded SoCs."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the fallacy that training cluster success transfers to edge hardware and evaluate total runtime memory components."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the pitfall of budgeting transformer serving memory from weights alone and analyze KV cache scaling with concurrency and context length.", "You can now analyze how changing execution regime can move the same transformer architecture between compute-leaning and bandwidth-leaning operating points.", "You can now evaluate why improved benchmark accuracy in a CNN-attention hybrid does not guarantee preserved throughput or memory efficiency.", "You can now analyze the fallacy that training cluster success transfers to edge hardware and evaluate total runtime memory components."]}], "id": "v1-06-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-06", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-06-99-test": {"title": "Test: Network Architectures", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-06-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "During autoregressive language model inference, single-token generation at batch size 1 achieves near-peak GPU floating-point throughput (TFLOP/s) because the matrix-vector multiplication is highly optimized.", "explanation": "False. The chapter's streamed-weight batch-1 decode model has low arithmetic intensity, so optimization alone does not guarantee near-peak compute throughput. Whether decode is bandwidth bound depends on the machine balance and realized traffic, and prefill or larger batches can reuse weights more effectively than single-token batch-1 generation.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why highly optimized batch-1 autoregressive decoding can still fall far below peak compute throughput under a low-intensity streamed-weight regime."]}, {"q": "The ____ hypothesis states that high-dimensional real-world data (such as natural images) actually resides on a much lower-dimensional structured surface embedded within the full input space, helping explain why useful structure can be learned from finite data.", "explanation": "manifold. The manifold hypothesis proposes that high-dimensional data lies near a lower-dimensional structured region in the ambient space, which helps explain why learning can exploit structure without searching the full input space.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["manifold"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the manifold hypothesis from its description and explain how it motivates learnability in high-dimensional settings."]}, {"q": "Why does position-free unmasked self-attention require explicit positional information supplied by mechanisms such as sinusoidal signals, learned positional embeddings, or related position-encoding schemes?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The section states that unmasked self-attention treats the sequence as an unordered set unless position information is injected: if the rows of $\\mathbf{X}$ are permuted, the output rows are permuted the same way. That is permutation equivariance, not invariance, and it is why positional information from embeddings, sinusoidal signals, or related mechanisms is needed to represent order. The hardware-padding and complexity answers are unrelated to the mathematical issue.", "type": "single", "options": ["Because matrix multiplication hardware cannot process tensors without fixed static padding across all dimensions.", "Because layer normalization removes the mean and variance of token vectors, destroying word identity.", "Because without positional information, unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant: permuting the input rows permutes the output rows in the same way, so the layer by itself does not encode order.", "Because positional encodings reduce the computational complexity of the attention matrix from $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(S)$."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain why unmasked self-attention is permutation equivariant and justify the role of positional information in sequence modeling."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Every family lowers onto GEMM**?", "options": ["The same accelerator is compute-, bandwidth-, or capacity-bound by architecture alone, so selection maps data symmetries to an operator family, filters candidates against storage, activation peak, intensity, latency, and power budgets, then validates on the target device.", "Factoring a convolution into depthwise and pointwise stages cuts parameters and multiply-accumulates by about 8-9x for 3x3 filters, but the factored layers have low arithmetic intensity, so far fewer FLOPs need not mean proportionally faster execution.", "Counting weights, multiply-accumulates, and resident bytes per building block predicts which iron-law term dominates: dense layers stream each weight once per batch, convolutions reuse each filter at every position, recurrence adds a serial floor, attention adds quadratic score work, and embedding lookups are copies.", "Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Every family lowers onto GEMM:** Convolution reaches matrix multiply through im2col or implicit GEMM, attention and LSTM gates fuse their projections into wide GEMMs, and pointwise convolutions and recommender MLPs are GEMMs, so GEMM dominates nearly every workload's operation count and the exceptions are the memory-bound remainder.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Every family lowers onto GEMM itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "Order the four primary computational stages executed during an end-to-end inference pass in a DLRM recommendation model:", "explanation": "The correct order is (1) and (2) in parallel -> (3) -> (4) (or (1) -> (2) -> (3) -> (4)). Step 1 and 2 are (1) Continuous features through Bottom MLP and (2) Categorical ID embedding table lookups. Step 3 is (3) Pairwise dot product feature interactions. Step 4 is (4) Top MLP classification for click-through rate prediction.", "type": "order", "items": ["Process continuous numerical features through the dense Bottom MLP to produce a dense representation", "Look up sparse categorical IDs across embedding tables to gather discrete embedding vectors", "Concatenate interaction dot products with Bottom MLP features and pass through the Top MLP to predict click-through probability"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze and sequence the four main pipeline stages of DLRM inference dataflow."]}, {"q": "In a Multi-Head Attention layer with model dimension $d_{\\text{model}} = 768$ and $N_{\\text{heads}} = 12$ heads, what is the per-head dimension $d_k$, and how does multi-head projection affect total computational FLOP complexity compared to a single attention head operating on the full 768 dimensions?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Multi-head attention partitions the model dimension across heads such that $d_k = d_{\\text{model}} / N_{\\text{heads}} = 768 / 12 = 64$. For $N_{\\text{heads}}$ heads, the $S \\times S$ score calculation requires $N_{\\text{heads}} \\times (S \\times S \\times d_k) = S^2 \\times (N_{\\text{heads}} \\cdot d_k) = S^2 \\cdot d_{\\text{model}}$ MACs, which is mathematically identical to the arithmetic cost of a single full-width attention head while providing the representational capacity of diverse attention subspaces. The claim that $d_k = 768$ misstates the per-head dimension. The claim of $d_k = 12$ inverts the head count and head dimension. The claim that multi-head attention eliminates the output projection matrix is false because $\\mathbf{W}^O$ is required to mix the concatenated head outputs.", "type": "single", "options": ["The per-head dimension is d_k = 768, increasing total projection FLOPs by \\$12× compared to a single head.", "The per-head dimension is $d_k = 768 / 12 = 64$; running 12 heads of dimension 64 has the exact same total projection and score FLOP complexity as a single head of dimension 768, while enabling the model to jointly attend to information from 12 distinct representation subspaces.", "The per-head dimension is d_k = 12, reducing total computational complexity by \\$64×.", "Multi-head attention eliminates the output projection matrix $\\mathbf{W}^O$, halving layer parameter count."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the dimension partitioning mechanics of multi-head attention and explain why multi-head projection preserves total computational complexity."]}, {"q": "The workload profile gives the transformer two rows, prefill and decode, even though both run the same weights. Under the table's representative reuse and streaming assumptions, which statement correctly explains why the limiting iron-law term can differ between them at small batch size?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The table is explicit that these are representative regimes under stated assumptions, not universal bottlenecks. In prefill, processing many prompt tokens together can reuse weights across the sequence and raise effective intensity enough for the compute term to matter; in small-batch decode, one-token-at-a-time execution rereads weights and accesses the KV cache with much lower reuse, so the data term is often the limiter. The capacity answer overstates a special case, and the serial-latency answer confuses decode with recurrent processing.", "type": "single", "options": ["Prefill is limited by the data term because it must read the whole prompt from storage, while decode is limited by the compute term because each token requires a full forward pass.", "Prefill processes all $S$ prompt tokens together, which can raise effective reuse and arithmetic intensity enough for the compute term to dominate under the stated assumptions; decode processes one token at a time with reread weights and a growing KV cache, so the data term often dominates at small batch.", "Both phases are limited by memory capacity, because the KV cache always exceeds device memory.", "Decode is compute bound because its $\\mathcal{O}(S^2)$ attention scores dominate, while prefill is latency bound because tokens are processed serially."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: use the workload-profile assumptions to predict why transformer prefill and decode can land on different sides of the iron law."]}, {"q": "In a deep CNN using stacked \\$3 × 3 convolutional filters with stride 1 and padding, by how much does the receptive field side length increase with each additional layer, and what is the architectural implication for detecting large objects?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Each successive \\$3 \\times 3$ stride-1 convolutional layer expands the receptive field side length by $K - 1 = 2$ pixels (layer 1 sees \\$3 \\times 3$, layer 2 sees \\$5 \\times 5$, layer 3 sees \\$7 \\times 7$). To detect large objects spanning 100+ pixels in a \\$224 \\times 224$ image, networks must either use deep stacks of layers, strided convolutions, or pooling operations. The claim of 9 pixels confuses kernel area with linear side growth. The claim of exponential doubling is incorrect for stride-1 layers. The claim that receptive field remains \\$3 \\times 3$ confuses single-layer kernel size with cumulative receptive field depth.", "type": "single", "options": ["Receptive field increases by 9 pixels per layer, allowing a 3-layer network to cover an entire \\$224 × 224 image.", "Receptive field side length doubles with each layer, scaling exponentially as \\$3^L.", "Receptive field side length grows linearly by 2 pixels per layer (a 3-layer stack sees a \\$7 \\times 7$ region), requiring deep stacks of layers or downsampling (pooling/striding) to detect objects spanning \\$100+$ pixels in high-resolution images.", "Receptive field remains strictly fixed at \\$3 × 3 across all layers because convolutional filter weights are shared across positions."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate receptive field growth across stacked convolutional layers and analyze the depth-versus-downsampling trade-off in network design."]}, {"q": "Order the sub-layer operations executed within a single standard Transformer encoder block during a forward pass:", "explanation": "The correct order is (1) -> (2) -> (3) -> (4) -> (5). Step 1 is (1) Linear Q, K, V projections. Step 2 is (2) Multi-head scaled dot-product attention. Step 3 is (3) First residual addition and layer normalization. Step 4 is (4) Position-wise feed-forward MLP. Step 5 is (5) Second residual addition and layer normalization.", "type": "order", "items": ["Project input activations into Query, Key, and Value tensors via linear weight matrices", "Compute multi-head scaled dot-product self-attention across all sequence positions", "Apply residual skip connection addition and layer normalization to the attention output", "Apply residual skip connection addition and layer normalization to the feed-forward output"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural layout and sub-layer execution order of a standard Transformer encoder block."]}, {"q": "A depthwise separable convolution decomposes standard convolution into two sequential operations: a ____ convolution that applies spatial filters to each input channel independently, followed by a \\$1 × 1 pointwise convolution that projects and mixes channels across the depth dimension.", "explanation": "depthwise. Depthwise separable convolution factorizes standard convolution into a depthwise convolution (spatial filtering per channel) and a pointwise convolution (\\$1 × 1 cross-channel linear combination).", "type": "fill", "answer": ["depthwise"], "hints": ["Think about: explain depthwise separable convolution components and explain how factorizing spatial and channel mixing reduces arithmetic complexity."]}, {"q": "In a standard RNN layer with input dimension $d_{\\text{in}} = 100$ and hidden state dimension $d_{\\text{hidden}} = 128$, how many total multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations are performed per sequence step to compute the unactivated hidden state?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. At each time step, the RNN performs two distinct matrix multiplications: the recurrent projection h_t-1 W_hh requires \\$128 × 128 = 16,384 MACs, and the input projection x_t W_hx requires \\$100 × 128 = 12,800 MACs. Together, these sum to \\$16,384 + 12,800 = 29,184 MACs per step per batch item. The choice of 12,800 MACs omits the recurrent projection. The choice of 1,280,000 MACs incorrectly assumes quadratic all-to-all history computation. The choice of 256 MACs confuses dimension addition with full matrix transformations.", "type": "single", "options": ["12,800 MACs, because only the input projection performs matrix multiplication.", "29,184 MACs, consisting of \\$128 \\times 128 = 16{,}384\\text{ MACs}$ for the recurrent projection plus \\$100 \\times 128 = 12{,}800\\text{ MACs}$ for the input projection.", "1,280,000 MACs, because recurrence multiplies all hidden states across all past time steps simultaneously.", "256 MACs, because an RNN updates only a single vector addition per step."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the per-step multiply-accumulate arithmetic cost of input and recurrent projections in an RNN layer."]}, {"q": "Because transformers offer superior parallelization and representational capacity for long-range dependencies, recurrent neural networks are entirely obsolete and have no valid deployment use cases in modern ML systems.", "explanation": "False. For streaming inference on resource-constrained microcontrollers (TinyML) and always-on audio devices with strict power and memory budgets, an RNN's constant $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ state memory footprint (e.g., 2 KiB for a 512-dimensional FP32 state) is vastly superior to the linear KV-cache growth and quadratic attention requirements of transformers, making RNNs a systems-justified choice.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: justify edge and streaming use cases where RNN constant-state memory is superior to transformer memory scaling."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-06-99-test", "chapter": "v1-06", "minutes": 10}});
